I've given your idea some thought, and I think potions are a better
idea than stones for a few reasons: potions of duplication wouldn't
require a new game mechanic (#rub'bing gems) and would therefore be
much more easily discoverable, and the quaffing/tossing mechanics are
very interesting.

At first I didn't see how duplication was a #dipping effect.  Usually
dipping an item into a potion affects that item directly.  But if
potions of duplication were made to behave like chameleon stones, then
the dipped item wouldn't chage; you dip an object in and pull two out,
the unaltered original and a copy.

But thinking about it this way leads me to (I think) a more appropriate
behavior: the one object indeed splits into two objects, and any
beneficial magic on the object is split up and divided randomly among
the two clones.  (As if the potion is able to duplicate the material,
but magic must be conserved.)

So a +3 rustproof dagger might split into a +2 dagger and a +1
rustproof dagger.  A magic marker (0:35) might split into two, (0:15)
and (0:20).  Dip a potion of foo into a potion of duplication, and you
get two diluted foo potions.  (Perhaps diluted potions cannot be
further duplicated.)  And so on.  I think also that quaffing should sap
you of half your hp and pw for the same reason (perhaps this percentage
is adjusted by the potion's B/C/U status).

I like this because it makes the potion less powerful than my stone
idea, in a neatly logical way.  I agree that, certainly, artifacts
should resist and resent the attempt.  Perhaps the artifact retains all
its magic, zaps you, and a cursed, negative enchanted clone is created.
 Invocation artifacts maybe should just make the potion boil and
explode in your hand.

With item cloning so weakened, the powerful use now seems to be cloning
creatures like nurses or foocubi or whatever.  A reasonable percentage
chance that the cloned creature is cancelled does a nice job of
balancing this, and perhaps isn't completely arbitrary (more of the law
of conservation of magic).

I'm not sure I like stack dipping only affecting a single dipped item.
It's inconsistent with !oPoly and !oHolyWater, and dipping a stack of
+3 darts into a stack of potions of duplication generates two new
objects (a +1 dart and a +2 dart) that won't stack in your inventory,
so now two items drop to the floor.  But I can't think of another
consistent way to handle this case, and being able to double a large
stack in size is probably broken.  (And maybe two objects dropping to
the floor isn't such a terrible outcome.  I only made chameleon stones
not stack for the cool effect of a stone keeping the same inventory
letter once it did its cloning magic.  But that's not needed here.)

Thanks for your feedback!
